Does build/ contain personal information?
Potentially, yes—mostly local machine information, not necessarily secrets. CMake-generated files, build logs, and debugging files can contain:

Absolute paths, potentially including your Windows username.
Compiler, SDK, and Visual Studio installation paths.
Local environment/configuration details.
Downloaded NVIDIA SDK files.
It doesn’t contain your display presets by design. Those live outside the project at %LOCALAPPDATA%\NvidiaQuickSwitch. The application also doesn’t deliberately collect credentials or tokens, but I wouldn’t publish the entire build directory.

Commit the source, tests, CMakeLists.txt, README.md, LICENSE, and .gitignore. If you want to distribute a compiled version, upload NvidiaQuickSwitch.exe and NVIDIA-NVAPI-LICENSE.txt as GitHub Release assets, rather than committing build/.

Note: .gitignore only prevents untracked files from being added; it doesn’t remove files already committed.